sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Animals from very distinct lines of descent can become adapted to similar conditions and so acquire a close external resemblance; such analogical or adaptive resemblances conceal rather than reveal blood-relationship, so they are almost worthless for classification between groups, although the same characters can indicate true affinity within a group.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Distinct lineages adapted to similar conditions converge in external form";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “Analogical or adaptive characters”. Scope: animals (and plants) of distinct lineages living under similar conditions"
}
